<decompilertest>
<binaryimage arch="x86:LE:64:default:gcc">
<!--
    looptest, ordering tests: a rotated counted loop whose latch tests `!=`
    behind a guard that tests an ordering.

    Issue: kuna-looptest (decbench O2: gcc tests `i != n` at the bottom of a
    rotated `for (i = 0; i < n; i++)`, behind `if (n > 0)`).  Stage P8
    (goto-quality), P0 assertion `option looptest binary|proven|assume`.

    The guard and the latch are not the same test as written: a negative n
    passes `i != n`.  When the carried value steps by a constant toward an
    invariant bound, starts strictly on the near side of it (the guard), and
    the bound is the start plus an exact multiple of the step, the value meets
    the bound and never passes it, so `i != n` is `i < n` on every iteration
    and the guard is the first test.

    Authored x86-64 (nasm), laid out as gcc -O2 emits it:
      down    if (n > 0) do { a[n] = 0; n -= 1; } while (n != 0);
      bytes   if (n > 0) { end = p + n; do *p++ = 0; while (p != end); }
              (n is a signed int; &p[n] > p is n > 0)
      step2   if (n > 0) { i = 0; do { a[i] = 0; i += 2; } while (i != n); }
              (an odd n never meets i, so the bottom test stays)
      wrapend if (n != 0) { end = a + (n - 1) + 1 in 32 bits; do ... }
              (at n == 0 the end is far past a: the guard is not a < end)

    Pass 1 (default `proven`): down prints `while (n > 0)`, bytes prints
    `while (p < end)`, both without the guard; step2 and wrapend stay guarded
    do-while loops.  Pass 2 (`option looptest binary`): down and bytes keep
    their guards and `!=` tests.
-->
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x100000" readonly="true">

</bytechunk>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x100040" name="down"/>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x100080" name="bytes"/>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x1000c0" name="step2"/>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x100100" name="wrapend"/>
</binaryimage>
<script>
  <com>parse line extern void down(int8 *a,int8 n);</com>
  <com>parse line extern void bytes(char *p,int4 n);</com>
  <com>parse line extern void step2(int8 *a,int8 n);</com>
  <com>parse line extern void wrapend(int8 *a,uint4 n);</com>
  <com>lo fu down</com>
  <com>decompile</com>
  <com>print C</com>
  <com>lo fu bytes</com>
  <com>decompile</com>
  <com>print C</com>
  <com>lo fu step2</com>
  <com>decompile</com>
  <com>print C</com>
  <com>lo fu wrapend</com>
  <com>decompile</com>
  <com>print C</com>
  <com>option looptest binary</com>
  <com>lo fu down</com>
  <com>decompile</com>
  <com>print C</com>
  <com>lo fu bytes</com>
  <com>decompile</com>
  <com>print C</com>
  <com>quit</com>
</script>
<stringmatch name="LOOPTEST-ORDER #1: down counts n to zero with the test at the top" min="1" max="1">^  while \(n &gt; 0\) \{$</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="LOOPTEST-ORDER #2: bytes walks p up to its end with the test at the top" min="1" max="1">^  while \(p &lt; v1\) \{$</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="LOOPTEST-ORDER #3: step2's i can step past n, so it stays a do-while" min="1" max="1">^    \} while \(v1 != n\);$</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="LOOPTEST-ORDER #4: wrapend's end is not a &lt; end at n == 0, so it stays a do-while" min="1" max="1">^    \} while \(a != v1\);$</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="LOOPTEST-ORDER #5: the n &gt; 0 guard prints for step2, and for down and bytes under binary" min="3" max="3">^  if \(1 &lt;= n\) \{$</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="LOOPTEST-ORDER #6: down keeps its != test under binary" min="1" max="1">^    \} while \(n\);$</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="LOOPTEST-ORDER #7: bytes keeps its != test under binary" min="1" max="1">^    \} while \(p != v1\);$</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="LOOPTEST-ORDER #8: wrapend keeps its n != 0 guard" min="1" max="1">^  if \(n\) \{$</stringmatch>
</decompilertest>
